.TH GITSPACE 1 "2026-11-27" "gitspace 0.8.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gitspace \- workspaces for git checkouts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gitspace
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gitspace
manages a set of bare mirrors, project definitions and on-disk workspaces.
A project is a named set of repositories. A topic is the piece of work. Their
combination is a workspace named
.IR project@topic .
Every owned member of a workspace is a git worktree of the repository mirror;
borrowed members are symlinks to existing checkouts.
.PP
With no arguments
.B gitspace
prints the inbox: workspaces and checkouts that need a decision.
Long operations print one progress line per item in text mode; JSON output is
silent and remains a single document.
.SH GLOSSARY
.TP
.B project
A named set of registered repositories, a branch template and a default ref.
Projects live in
.IR <data>/projects.toml .
.TP
.B repo
A registered repository identity and its bare mirror under
.IR <data>/repos/<sha256>.git .
The identity is immutable; the fetch and push URLs are not.
.TP
.B checkout
A working tree or clone. Owned checkouts are worktrees of a mirror; external
checkouts are indexed in place.
.TP
.B workspace
An instance of a project at one topic, stored under
.IR <projects.root>/<project>/<topic> .
.TP
.B member
A repository's role in a workspace. A member is owned or borrowed.
.SH TARGETS
Most commands accept one of the following targets. Ambiguous or missing
targets are errors, never guesses.
.TP
.B workspace
.IR project@topic ,
for example
.BR vemags@4711 .
.TP
.B workspace:member
One member, for example
.BR vemags@4711:api .
.TP
.B checkout
An absolute or relative path to an indexed checkout. A path inside a checkout
resolves to that checkout.
.TP
.B repo:<alias>
A repository by alias, for example
.BR repo:vemags .
.TP
.B project:<name>
A project by name, for example
.BR project:vemags .
.TP
.B project/topic[/member]
A layout-shaped target, for example
.BR vemags/4711/api .
.PP
Repository aliases and unique substrings are accepted where a repository is
expected. An ambiguous substring is an error.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Inspect
.TP
.B gitspace ls
List indexed checkouts. Filters:
.BR \-\-repo ,
.BR \-\-project ,
.BR \-\-topic ,
.BR \-\-workspace ,
.BR \-\-label ,
.BR \-\-state ,
.BR \-\-kind ,
.BR \-\-source .
.B \-\-refresh
recomputes the cached git status without fetching.
.B \-\-verbose
adds the mirror hash and path.
.TP
.B gitspace show TARGET
Print the identity and definition of a workspace, checkout, project or
repository. No live git state is included; use
.B status
for that.
.TP
.B gitspace status TARGET
Print the live state: dirty and untracked files, ahead/behind, unpushed
commits, an operation in progress and risk reasons.
.B \-\-long
lists changed and untracked paths;
.B \-\-diff
adds the staged and unstaged patch and implies
.BR \-\-long .
.B \-\-diff\-lines
limits the patch. These options need a single checkout or member target.
.TP
.B gitspace path TARGET
Print the target's path and nothing else. A repository target needs exactly
one checkout.
.TP
.B gitspace log TARGET
Show outgoing and incoming commits. Outgoing is what a push would send;
incoming is what a rebase would integrate.
.BR \-\-outgoing ,
.BR \-\-incoming ,
.BR \-\-both ,
.BR \-\-limit ,
.B \-\-all
and
.B \-\-fetch
control the view.
.SS Create and change
.TP
.B gitspace checkout PROJECT[@REF] [\-\-topic T]
Check out a project topic. Without an explicit topic the ref is checked out
as-is. A topic derives an owned branch from the branch template (default
.IR feature/{topic} ).
The topic is used verbatim in the branch, including
.BR / ;
only the workspace directory is slugged. Without a ref the branch starts at
each repository's default ref.
.B \-\-as\-is
checks the ref out unchanged;
.B \-\-branch
overrides the derived name;
.B \-\-detach
creates a detached checkout.
.B co
is an alias for
.BR checkout .
.TP
.B gitspace workspace new [PROJECT@]TOPIC [\-\-own REPO[@REF]]...
Create a workspace with explicit owned and borrowed members.
.B \-\-borrow
symlinks an existing checkout instead of creating a worktree.
.B \-\-as
chooses a member directory.
.B \-\-label
and
.B \-\-description
set workspace metadata.
.B \-\-fetch
updates the mirrors first. Re-running is ensure-only: existing members are
kept and missing members are added.
.TP
.B gitspace workspace add WORKSPACE [\-\-own REPO[@REF]]...
Add missing members to an existing workspace. The workspace must already
exist; a typo fails instead of creating a second workspace. Nothing existing
is changed.
.TP
.B gitspace workspace label add WORKSPACE LABEL...
Add workspace labels.
.TP
.B gitspace workspace label rm WORKSPACE LABEL...
Remove individual labels.
.TP
.B gitspace workspace label set WORKSPACE [LABEL...]
Replace the whole label set.
.TP
.B gitspace workspace label ls WORKSPACE
List workspace labels.
.PP
Manage workspace labels. Labels are free-form, trimmed and unique. Adding is a
union; removing removes individual labels; setting replaces the whole set.
Labels live in the workspace marker and survive a reindex.
.TP
.B gitspace workspace edit WORKSPACE [\-\-description D|--clear-description]
Change the workspace description.
.TP
.B gitspace workspace ls
List workspaces.
.B \-\-project
and
.B \-\-label
filter;
.B \-\-all
includes torn-down workspaces.
.TP
.B gitspace workspace rm WORKSPACE[:MEMBER] [\-\-force] [\-\-delete-branch]
Remove a workspace or one member. Owned members are removed as worktrees;
borrowed members are unlinked, never deleted. Risky members are refused unless
.B \-\-force
archives their work under
.IR <data>/trash .
.B \-\-delete-branch
also deletes owned local branches whose tip is already reachable from a
remote, a tag or another local branch. The default branch and branches checked
out elsewhere are never deleted. With
.BR \-\-force ,
a branch with local-only commits is anchored under
.IR refs/gitspace/keep/branch/ ... 
first.
.TP
.B gitspace project new NAME [\-\-repo ALIAS]...
Create a project definition.
.B \-\-at
chooses the project directory;
.B \-\-branch-template
and
.B \-\-default-ref
set the derivation defaults.
.TP
.B gitspace project ls
List projects.
.TP
.B gitspace project edit NAME [\-\-description D|--clear-description]
Change the project description.
.TP
.B gitspace project add NAME REPO...
Add future project members.
.TP
.B gitspace project remove NAME REPO...
Remove future project members.
.PP
Change future project membership. Existing workspaces are never moved.
.TP
.B gitspace project rm NAME [\-\-force]
Remove the project definition. Refuses while workspaces exist;
.B \-\-force
keeps every checkout directory in place.
.TP
.B gitspace repo init NAME
Create an empty local repository, its implicit project and the first
checkout.
.TP
.B gitspace repo import PATH [\-\-name NAME]
Adopt a local clone without a remote as a local repository, copying its
history into a bare mirror.
.TP
.B gitspace repo add URL|PATH [\-\-name NAME] [\-\-remote NAME]
Register a remote-backed repository and create its mirror. A path uses one of
the checkout's remotes as the URL; the checkout itself is not indexed.
.TP
.B gitspace repo ls
List registered repositories and their checkouts.
.TP
.B gitspace repo branches REPO
List the repository's local and remote branches and, with
.BR \-\-tags ,
tags. The default and checked-out branches are marked.
.B \-\-match
takes a glob;
.B \-\-regex
an Elixir regular expression.
.TP
.B gitspace repo fetch REPO|--all
Fetch one repository or every repository. One progress line is printed per
repository as it completes. Without a target and without
.BR \-\-all ,
the command is a usage error.
.TP
.B gitspace repo rm REPO [\-\-force] [\-\-dry-run]
Remove a mirror. A local repository is bundled under
.IR <data>/trash/repos/
first.
.TP
.B gitspace repo alias REPO NAME
Change the repository alias.
.TP
.B gitspace repo set-url REPO URL [\-\-push]
Change the fetch URL, or the push URL with
.BR \-\-push .
The identity and mirror directory never change.
.SS Work
.TP
.B gitspace push TARGET [\-\-dry-run] [\-\-remote NAME] [\-\-force|--force-with-lease]
Push the checked-out branch to the same-named remote branch.
.B \-\-force-with-lease
is the recommended choice after a rebase: the push is rejected when the local
remote-tracking ref no longer matches the remote. Fetch first for a current
lease.
.B \-\-force
skips that check and can overwrite remote work. The flags are mutually
exclusive.
.TP
.B gitspace rebase [TARGET] [\-\-fetch|--continue|--abort]
Rebase each worktree onto the ref it was derived from, falling back to its
configured upstream for plain checkouts. A branch whose push target does not
exist yet is reported up to date. Conflicts are left in place and the
remaining worktrees are still attempted.
.TP
.B gitspace base TARGET [REF|--clear]
Show or change the ref a checkout rebases onto. The base is recorded in
.IR branch.<name>.gitspace\-base ;
it does not change the push target. Nothing is fetched or rebased.
.SS Maintain
.TP
.B gitspace doctor check
Check git, paths, the index, repositories, mirrors, checkouts, workspaces,
keep refs and sync roots. Plain
.B gitspace doctor
is the same check.
.TP
.B gitspace doctor fix
Repair mirrors and paths, prune redundant keep anchors and fix other
recoverable problems.
.TP
.B gitspace doctor reindex
Rebuild the index from mirrors and workspace markers.
.TP
.B gitspace doctor keep ls
List
.IR refs/gitspace/keep/
anchors.
.TP
.B gitspace doctor keep prune [\-\-older-than DAYS] [\-\-dry-run] [\-\-gc] [\-\-yes]
Prune redundant anchors; with
.B \-\-older-than
also prune orphaned anchors.
.TP
.B gitspace setup [\-\-data DIR] [\-\-projects DIR]
Write the initial configuration.
.TP
.B gitspace config list
Show the effective configuration.
.TP
.B gitspace config get KEY
Read one setting.
.TP
.B gitspace config set KEY VALUE [\-\-force]
Change one setting.
.TP
.B gitspace config export [\-\-output FILE]
Write a versioned JSON bundle of projects and remote-backed repositories.
Workspaces, checkouts, labels, absolute paths and credentials are excluded;
local repositories are skipped. The bundle is written to stdout unless
.B \-\-output
is given.
.TP
.B gitspace config import FILE|-
Create missing repositories and projects from a bundle. Existing entries are
left untouched, even when their members or metadata differ. A project whose
member was skipped or missing is created without that member and the import is
partial.
.B \-\-dry-run
only prints the plan;
.B \-\-no-fetch
registers repositories without fetching; without a terminal
.B \-\-yes
is required.
.TP
.B gitspace scan [PATH...] [\-\-max-depth N] [\-\-exclude GLOB]
Report existing clones and worktrees without changing anything.
.TP
.B gitspace link PATH [\-\-repo NAME]
Index an existing checkout as an external checkout.
.TP
.B gitspace unlink PATH
Forget an indexed external checkout.
.TP
.B gitspace completion bash
Print the bash completion script.
.TP
.B gitspace completion zsh
Print the zsh completion script.
.TP
.B gitspace completion fish
Print the fish completion script.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the machine-readable envelope. Stable fields are documented per command.
.TP
.B \-\-no-color
Disable colored human output.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Print git argv and add extra metadata where supported.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Answer confirmation prompts. Required for destructive or partial operations
without a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-refresh
Recompute cached local git status. Never fetches.
.TP
.B \-\-fetch
Update mirrors from their remotes. Requires network access.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Unclassified error.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
Target not found, or ambiguous.
.TP
.B 4
Conflict, including an occupied branch or an occupied path.
.TP
.B 5
Refused for safety.
.TP
.B 6
Confirmation required.
.TP
.B 7
Partial success or invalid configuration.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <config>
TOML configuration at
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gitspace/config.toml ,
or
.IR $GITSPACE_CONFIG .
.TP
.I <data>/projects.toml
Project definitions.
.TP
.I <data>/index.sqlite3
The SQLite index.
.TP
.I <data>/repos/
Bare mirrors named by the SHA-256 of the repository identity, plus
.IR remotes.txt .
.TP
.I <projects.root>/<project>/<topic>/
Workspace directory and
.IR .gitspace.json .
.TP
.I <data>/trash/
Archived worktrees, untracked files and local-repository bundles.
.TP
.I <data>/rebase.json
Journal for rolling back completed rebases.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GITSPACE_CONFIG
Alternative configuration file.
.TP
.B GITSPACE_DATA
Override the data directory.
.TP
.B GITSPACE_PROJECTS_ROOT
Override the projects root.
.TP
.B GITSPACE_NO_PROMPT
Disable interactive confirmation even on a terminal.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start a topic from main:
.nf
gitspace checkout vemags@main \-\-topic MT4711
.fi
.PP
Work on the current checkout:
.nf
gitspace show .
gitspace status . \-\-long
gitspace log . \-\-incoming
.fi
.PP
Finish after a rebase:
.nf
gitspace rebase vemags@MT4711 \-\-fetch
gitspace push vemags@MT4711:api \-\-force-with-lease
.fi
.PP
Share projects and repositories:
.nf
gitspace config export \-\-output gitspace-bundle.json
gitspace config import gitspace-bundle.json \-\-yes
.fi
.PP
Repair an installation:
.nf
gitspace doctor
gitspace doctor fix
gitspace doctor reindex
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR git-worktree (1),
.BR git-rebase (1)
